{"results":[{"url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DfJ7X69EtRJ/","final_url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DfJ7X69EtRJ/","title":"The Siasat Daily on Instagram: \"Hyderabad is rationing piped water to once every three days and fining pubs up to Rs 20,000 for washing floors with drinking water. But videos of water gushing onto streets in two localities have left residents asking who will answer for those leaks.\n\nIn one video, from Maula Ali, Manjeera water is seen running down a road. In the other, from SR Nagar, water spills from a pump outside JJ Hospital.\n\nBoth clips spread on social media, with users asking who was responsible for letting water go to waste in the middle of a crisis.\n\n“Restaurants and pubs were asked to pay a Rs 20,000 fine for wasting water. Who should be penalised for this pump, which is leaking water?” asked Mohammed Ilyas, who posted the SR Nagar video on Instagram.\n\nFines for some:\nThe Hyderabad Metropolitan Water Supply and Sewerage Board (HMWSSB) has been fining people, restaurants and pubs between Rs 5,000 and Rs 20,000 for wasting water while cleaning their premises. One city pub was recently fined Rs 20,000.\n\nFrom November 1, water tankers caught spilling drinking water on the way will also be fined Rs 5,000. HMWSSB Managing Director K Ashok Reddy issued the order on October 24 after the board found tankers being overfilled and water splashing onto roads.\n\nTankers with leaks, missing lids or loose covers will not be filled until they are repaired.\n\nThe Maula Ali leak has struck a nerve because the Manjeera is one of the sources under the most strain. The board expects a shortfall of about 100 million gallons a day (MGD) from Singur and Manjeera between January and August.\n\nRead full story on siasat.com\"","description":"Hyderabad is rationing piped water to once every three days and fining pubs up to Rs 20,000 for washing floors with drinking water. But videos of water gushing onto streets in two localities have left residents asking who will answer for those leaks.\n\nIn one video, from Maula Ali, Manjeera water is seen running down a road. In the other, from SR Nagar, water spills from a pump outside JJ Hospital.\n\nBoth clips spread on social media, with users asking who was responsible for letting water go to waste in the middle of a crisis.\n\n“Restaurants and pubs were asked to pay a Rs 20,000 fine for wasting water. Who should be penalised for this pump, which is leaking water?” asked Mohammed Ilyas, who posted the SR Nagar video on Instagram.\n\nFines for some:\nThe Hyderabad Metropolitan Water Supply and Sewerage Board (HMWSSB) has been fining people, restaurants and pubs between Rs 5,000 and Rs 20,000 for wasting water while cleaning their premises. One city pub was recently fined Rs 20,000.\n\nFrom November 1, water tankers caught spilling drinking water on the way will also be fined Rs 5,000. HMWSSB Managing Director K Ashok Reddy issued the order on October 24 after the board found tankers being overfilled and water splashing onto roads.\n\nTankers with leaks, missing lids or loose covers will not be filled until they are repaired.\n\nThe Maula Ali leak has struck a nerve because the Manjeera is one of the sources under the most strain. The board expects a shortfall of about 100 million gallons a day (MGD) from Singur and Manjeera between January and August.\n\nRead full story on siasat.com - @siasatdaily on October 31, 2026","language":"en","text":"Hyderabad is rationing piped water to once every three days and fining pubs up to Rs 20,000 for washing floors with drinking water. But videos of water gushing onto streets in two localities have left residents asking who will answer for those leaks.  \n  \nIn one video, from Maula Ali, Manjeera water is seen running down a road. In the other, from SR Nagar, water spills from a pump outside JJ Hospital.  \n  \nBoth clips spread on social media, with users asking who was responsible for letting water go to waste in the middle of a crisis.  \n  \n“Restaurants and pubs were asked to pay a Rs 20,000 fine for wasting water. Who should be penalised for this pump, which is leaking water?” asked Mohammed Ilyas, who posted the SR Nagar video on Instagram.  \n  \nFines for some:  \nThe Hyderabad Metropolitan Water Supply and Sewerage Board (HMWSSB) has been fining people, restaurants and pubs between Rs 5,000 and Rs 20,000 for wasting water while cleaning their premises. One city pub was recently fined Rs 20,000.  \n  \nFrom November 1, water tankers caught spilling drinking water on the way will also be fined Rs 5,000. HMWSSB Managing Director K Ashok Reddy issued the order on October 24 after the board found tankers being overfilled and water splashing onto roads.  \n  \nTankers with leaks, missing lids or loose covers will not be filled until they are repaired.  \n  \nThe Maula Ali leak has struck a nerve because the Manjeera is one of the sources under the most strain. The board expects a shortfall of about 100 million gallons a day (MGD) from Singur and Manjeera between January and August.  \n  \nRead full story on siasat.com\n\nIn one video, from Maula Ali, Manjeera water is seen running down a road. In the other, from SR Nagar, water spills from a pump outside JJ Hospital.\n\nBoth clips spread on social media, with users asking who was responsible for letting water go to waste in the middle of a crisis.\n\n“Restaurants and pubs were asked to pay a Rs 20,000 fine for wasting water. Who should be penalised for this pump, which is leaking water?” asked Mohammed Ilyas, who posted the SR Nagar video on Instagram.\n\nFines for some:\n\nThe Hyderabad Metropolitan Water Supply and Sewerage Board (HMWSSB) has been fining people, restaurants and pubs between Rs 5,000 and Rs 20,000 for wasting water while cleaning their premises. One city pub was recently fined Rs 20,000.\n\nFrom November 1, water tankers caught spilling drinking water on the way will also be fined Rs 5,000. HMWSSB Managing Director K Ashok Reddy issued the order on October 24 after the board found tankers being overfilled and water splashing onto roads.\n\nTankers with leaks, missing lids or loose covers will not be filled until they are repaired.\n\nThe Maula Ali leak has struck a nerve because the Manjeera is one of the sources under the most strain. The board expects a shortfall of about 100 million gallons a day (MGD) from Singur and Manjeera between January and August.\n\nRead full story on siasat.com\n\nMeraj colony, SS CONVENTION k samney se takriban 2 saal se paani sirf behra, koi audedaar zimedari se kaam nahi karey aaj tak. @siasatdaily please report with video proof\n\nReply\n\nBohot jagah ye ich haal hai bhai humare area Jeedimetla me 6 mahine se jara lakin complaints kare baad bhi action nai lare\n\nReply\n\n@cmc\\_offcl @ghmc.official endi sir idhi. 👏😂\n\nReply\n\nReply\n\n#DeclareSiddipetcapitalofTelangana #ChangeCapitalofTelangana #GoBckNonTspiples #GoBckMNC #GoBckWesternCulture Jai Telangana Jai Jai Telangana................. who supports nonts piples in ts we boycott them and there parties too...  \nwe dont want ur assets & liabilities take it back withu to ur native place.\n\nwe dont want ur assets & liabilities take it back withu to ur native place.\n\n1 like\n\nReply\n\n@hmwssb\\_online will your department pay fine?\n\nReply\n\n@revanth\\_reddy\\_official\\_\\_ @ghmc.official kindly work on the rain water harvesting.. When the city is receiving rains you should pre plan on the storage.\n\n1 like\n\nReply\n\nYe log to sir residents ko pareshan karte..\n\nReply\n\n@mohammed\\_ilyas554 Good Question Sir 🫡 🙌\n\nReply\n\nReply\n\nLog in to like or comment.","author":null,"published_date":"2026-10-31T10:19:56.000Z","latency_ms":6718.993189002504,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}